  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-02-06.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="050630"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#050630" label="[05:06:30]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Fiwix HN discussion <a rel="nofollow" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42947383">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42947383</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="050642"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#050642" label="[05:06:42]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION mentioned bootstrappable.org </span></div><div class="line" id="073543"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#073543" label="[07:35:43]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pabs3: I appreciate your comments, thank you!</span></div><div class="line" id="075238"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#075238" label="[07:52:38]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't understand the order of the comments: a recent comment from you is placed at the top and another one also recent, almost at the bottom</span></div><div class="line" id="083228"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083228" label="[08:32:28]">&lt;nimaje&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for one, comments can be a reply to another comment, so you have a tree structure there, additionally there is a voting system for comments and highly voted comments go to the top</span></div><div class="line" id="083721"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083721" label="[08:37:21]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nimaje: ah, perhaps I don't see the votes for each comment because I'm not registered</span></div><div class="line" id="084532"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#084532" label="[08:45:32]">&lt;nimaje&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no idea if anyone can see them or if they are just used internally, what you can see is the upvote button (the triangle at the start of the comment) and if some coment gets downvoted too much it will get grayer and grayer and eventually filtered (I don't have a hn account too, but read there sometimes)</span></div><div class="line" id="084906"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#084906" label="[08:49:06]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I see</span></div><div class="line" id="142431"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142431" label="[14:24:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">seems, ~20years ago, C++ dependencies had remained cleanly separated from base system components written in C</span></div><div class="line" id="142544"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142544" label="[14:25:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GNU texinfo is just another example of important components that are affected by this</span></div><div class="line" id="142649"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142649" label="[14:26:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, i can't comment on fiwix, since i don't use any &quot;modern&quot; web-browser either</span></div><div class="line" id="142755"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142755" label="[14:27:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the 16bit real-mode bootcode/bios issues aren't specific to fiwix, no clue why this argument got used to critize fiwix</span></div><div class="line" id="142839"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142839" label="[14:28:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to advertize risc-v, isn't commonly available, why 99% of all commodity hardware can run i386 fiwix bare metal</span></div><div class="line" id="142929"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142929" label="[14:29:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and to my knowledge risc-v hasn't got a _complete_ bootstrapping pather either, to avoid i386 being involved as development host towards risc-v</span></div><div class="line" id="143334"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#143334" label="[14:33:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's interesting however, for one or another the MIT v6 system retained PDP-11 target support instead of x86, until year 2006</span></div><div class="line" id="143642"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#143642" label="[14:36:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*while 99% of all commodity hardware (coffee didn't make it into my brain yet)</span></div><div class="line" id="143740"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#143740" label="[14:37:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, at Telekom University they had decided to throw Motorola ASM at the students</span></div><div class="line" id="143935"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#143935" label="[14:39:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without any BIOS of cause, and beginning with initializing basic adress registers and similar</span></div><div class="line" id="144134"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144134" label="[14:41:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then again, university didn't bother with system integration concerns and bootstrapping paths</span></div><div class="line" id="144217"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144217" label="[14:42:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for digital circuit design you've just had to install some of their Altera Studio onto some micros~1 vista machine</span></div><div class="line" id="144312"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144312" label="[14:43:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">only a few guys were smart enough, to proceed with their digital circuit design training with pencil and paper, connecting a handfull of PALs and GALs</span></div><div class="line" id="144550"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144550" label="[14:45:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i suspect, when switching from PDP-11 type systems to x86/i386, at university, a complete bootstrapping path that includes hardware-design is lost</span></div><div class="line" id="144714"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144714" label="[14:47:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a thing such as Gigatron TTL can be designed and constructed from scratch onto a breadboard, without relying on gigantic IDEs</span></div><div class="line" id="144719"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144719" label="[14:47:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*for one or another reason</span></div><div class="line" id="145217"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145217" label="[14:52:17]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">risc-v full bootstrap path should be possible, at least with considering flashing the bootchip to be a pre-bootstrap phase as x86 also gets away with a full on BIOS (or worse a UEFI system)</span></div><div class="line" id="145355"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145355" label="[14:53:55]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Part of that being thanks to u-boot (which you put on the bootchip) effectively allowing to load an extlinux/extlinux.conf file</span></div><div class="line" id="145413"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145413" label="[14:54:13]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">cf. <a rel="nofollow" href="https://docs.u-boot.org/en/latest/develop/bootstd/overview.html">https://docs.u-boot.org/en/latest/develop/bootstd/overview.html</a>  and <a rel="nofollow" href="https://docs.u-boot.org/en/latest/develop/distro.html">https://docs.u-boot.org/en/latest/develop/distro.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="145513"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#145513" label="[14:55:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my knowledge, there isn't complete (posix) toolchain and kernel support for risc-v, that avoided c++ (as an initial criteria)</span></div><div class="line" id="145557"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#145557" label="[14:55:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tinycc/risc-v (another simplified criteria to look at) isn't complete yet, and you couldn't compile a linux kernel with it anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="145642"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#145642" label="[14:56:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">u-boot introduces a gigantic dependency graph circular dependency; finally, that's what i fear, RISC-V adds up compexity atop x86/i386</span></div><div class="line" id="145716"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145716" label="[14:57:16]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tcc mob works for riscv with making sure you don't pass -g</span></div><div class="line" id="145735"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145735" label="[14:57:35]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And there's alloca missing on riscv but that's non-standard</span></div><div class="line" id="145802"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#145802" label="[14:58:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">excellent, but you couldn't compile a linux-kernel version needed... last linux kernel version that i could confirm was linux-2.4</span></div><div class="line" id="145832"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#145832" label="[14:58:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and that one didn't have risc-v on board; of cause there might be any other POSIX kernel for risc-v that tinycc could digest sources for</span></div><div class="line" id="145834"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145834" label="[14:58:34]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what's missing on riscv is an early kernel like builder-hex0 or posix-runner</span></div><div class="line" id="145842"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145842" label="[14:58:42]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and also possibly an intermediate one like Fiwix</span></div><div class="line" id="145847"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145847" label="[14:58:47]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah kernel I haven't tried, so far just alpine's kernel + bootstrap-initrd and I only get stuck at compiling git due to lack of alloca</span></div><div class="line" id="145854"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#145854" label="[14:58:54]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(and GNU make)</span></div><div class="line" id="145942"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#145942" label="[14:59:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">kernel, bootloader, firmware... there is a potential gigantic circular dependency to crack open, with risc-v, and this too implies avoiding i386 as intermediate development host</span></div><div class="line" id="150119"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150119" label="[15:01:19]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well bootloader is droppable, check u-boot docs really and that's the BIOS-equivalent stuff</span></div><div class="line" id="150201"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150201" label="[15:02:01]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Otherwise it would be comparable to bootstrapping on non-PC x86 like the PS4</span></div><div class="line" id="150250"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#150250" label="[15:02:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i got a prognosis for risc-v, that is it will end up just like aarch32/64 did, without a complete bootstrapping path, and the need for most recent kernel and toolchain versions</span></div><div class="line" id="150342"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150342" label="[15:03:42]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well arm is one where even just getting mainline linux to boot up regardless of bootstrapping is a challenge</span></div><div class="line" id="150408"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150408" label="[15:04:08]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and Arm at least has the SystemReady standards, most boards just don't care about them</span></div><div class="line" id="150416"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150416" label="[15:04:16]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK risc-v has nothing similar</span></div><div class="line" id="150509"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150509" label="[15:05:09]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">risc-v entirely has systemready equivalent and effectively that's why VisionFive2 can just boot up full mainline in a generic manner</span></div><div class="line" id="150549"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150549" label="[15:05:49]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">on x86, we're currently relying on residual &quot;IBM compatibility&quot; to simplify things (e.g. Fiwix expects IDE controllers at fixed addresses), something other archs never had</span></div><div class="line" id="150619"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150619" label="[15:06:19]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Even platforms like sun sparc and macppc?</span></div><div class="line" id="150641"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150641" label="[15:06:41]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK those used OpenFirmware, which is where DT comes from</span></div><div class="line" id="150659"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150659" label="[15:06:59]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="150701"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150701" label="[15:07:01]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so any risc-v bootstrap would need to implement DT or ACPI parsing even in the &quot;simple&quot; kernels</span></div><div class="line" id="150714"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150714" label="[15:07:14]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or just be board-specific</span></div><div class="line" id="150807"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150807" label="[15:08:07]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the only reason we can &quot;cheat&quot; on x86 is because OEMs copied IBM's address space layout, rather than adding a description language</span></div><div class="line" id="150818"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150818" label="[15:08:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and ACPI then standardized much of that as &quot;fixed devices&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="150850"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150850" label="[15:08:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">any ACPI system without &quot;fixed devices&quot; is &quot;hardware-reduced&quot;, including all non-x86 systems</span></div><div class="line" id="150902"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#150902" label="[15:09:02]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure enough, we can't currently complete a bootstrap on a &quot;hardware-reduced&quot; system</span></div><div class="line" id="150939"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150939" label="[15:09:39]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Although in the case of riscv maybe SBI is effectively enough of an interface <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/riscv-non-isa/riscv-sbi-doc">https://github.com/riscv-non-isa/riscv-sbi-doc</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="150950"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#150950" label="[15:09:50]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">At least for very early bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="151138"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#151138" label="[15:11:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">interestingly, the linux2.4/tinycc kernel that i salvaged does boot on various x86 hardware, without ACPI support</span></div><div class="line" id="151157"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#151157" label="[15:11:57]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Maybe it was APM at the time?</span></div><div class="line" id="151205"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#151205" label="[15:12:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ioAPIC is needed for some USB/SATA controllers</span></div><div class="line" id="151319"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#151319" label="[15:13:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my understanding, APM and ACPI aren't equivalent, and i don't care if i have to cold-reset a system at the power-plug</span></div><div class="line" id="151358"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#151358" label="[15:13:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the linux2.4 kernel even initializes SMP, without the presence of ACPI</span></div><div class="line" id="151603"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#151603" label="[15:16:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, i've had my fun with aarch32/64, fiddling together some custom firmware for rockchip/pro devices</span></div><div class="line" id="151742"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#151742" label="[15:17:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and such type of system will _force_ you to update kernel/toolchain</span></div><div class="line" id="151753"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#151753" label="[15:17:53]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah I just wouldn't put riscv in the same category as arm, specially rockchip where you're typically stuck to vendor kernels (like my rockpro64 annoyingly seems to be)</span></div><div class="line" id="152012"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152012" label="[15:20:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a rather simple criteria to have a look at: avoiding c++ completely, as either build-time or run-time dependence, direct or transitive</span></div><div class="line" id="152046"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152046" label="[15:20:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">c++ must remain optional, and currently it isn't with either aarch32/64 nor riscv, if you wanted to end up with a posix development host</span></div><div class="line" id="152147"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152147" label="[15:21:47]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Even with considering that opensbi+u-boot can just be on the bootchip and so get a bios exception?</span></div><div class="line" id="152228"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152228" label="[15:22:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">last time i had a look at uboot, that introduced python/swig/c++ as build-time dependency, which can be fixed, but it's not trivial</span></div><div class="line" id="152302"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152302" label="[15:23:02]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah but coreboot has the exact same problems if not worse</span></div><div class="line" id="152338"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152338" label="[15:23:38]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And I doubt Tianocore/edk2 is much better and that's just a bootloader.</span></div><div class="line" id="152428"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152428" label="[15:24:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if at all some coreboot support was available, exactly, and i haven't tried yet if at least tinycc could be hooked into</span></div><div class="line" id="152503"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152503" label="[15:25:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to compile coreboot itself</span></div><div class="line" id="152543"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152543" label="[15:25:43]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">syslinux did pass with tinycc, but that's only some bootloader, not the bios/firmware</span></div><div class="line" id="152631"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152631" label="[15:26:31]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah you'd need to compile seabios for this, otherwise the bootstrap would be different</span></div><div class="line" id="152705"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152705" label="[15:27:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and this too implies a circular dependency tinycc/tccboot &lt;--&gt; syslinux &lt;--&gt; tinycc/tccboot; simplified speaking</span></div><div class="line" id="152741"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152741" label="[15:27:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aarch32/64 and risc-v aren't much different, at least their uboot firmware is opensource, but it's not bootstrappable</span></div><div class="line" id="152953"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#152953" label="[15:29:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i suspect MIT must have had their reasons, to keep their v6 PDP-11 on university schedule</span></div><div class="line" id="153235"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153235" label="[15:32:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">until year 2006, and then switched to vx6</span></div><div class="line" id="153406"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153406" label="[15:34:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">recently they've ported it to risc-v, but i doubt the hardware design and implementation could have remained covered as it was with their PDP-11 variant</span></div><div class="line" id="153417"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153417" label="[15:34:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/2012/xv6.html">https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/2012/xv6.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="153513"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153513" label="[15:35:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/2024/xv6.html">https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/2024/xv6.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="153542"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153542" label="[15:35:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my understanding MIT argument was, students were overloaded with PDP-11 and X86 lectured in parallel</span></div><div class="line" id="153604"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153604" label="[15:36:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i had quit at Telekom University 2005, for some reason</span></div><div class="line" id="153628"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153628" label="[15:36:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and nowadays receive regular blackmail and extortion letters from bureaucracy</span></div><div class="line" id="153858"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153858" label="[15:38:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the c++ madness too is something we've had to thank telekom for</span></div><div class="line" id="154005"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154005" label="[15:40:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, there was _two_ &quot;scientific calculator&quot; series from texas instruments</span></div><div class="line" id="154041"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154041" label="[15:40:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one was built upon Z80 (TI83), and the &quot;superior&quot; design had chosen Motorola (TI89)</span></div><div class="line" id="154141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154141" label="[15:41:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my knowledge the Z80 series remained in prodcution, while the superio motorala design one went EOL</span></div><div class="line" id="154157"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154157" label="[15:41:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">z80, that's the infamous 16bit real-mode</span></div><div class="line" id="154344"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154344" label="[15:43:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nowadays those TI scientific calculators are equipped with ARM, i couldn't care less</span></div><div class="line" id="154415"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154415" label="[15:44:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the superior motorola design already surpassed my intellect by far already</span></div><div class="line" id="154604"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154604" label="[15:46:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although we too covered digital circuit design, signal processing, and a gigangic load of other things, in three years, as a few other &quot;hobbies&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="154613"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154613" label="[15:46:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">compensated _below_ minimum wage</span></div><div class="line" id="154701"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154701" label="[15:47:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the last remaining telekom unversity in germany closed year 2021, 15 years after i had left</span></div><div class="line" id="154731"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154731" label="[15:47:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">don't ask me what happened to Bell Labs, or any other of such entitites</span></div><div class="line" id="154754"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154754" label="[15:47:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my knowledge the computer algebra system of TI83 and TI89 had never been opensourced</span></div><div class="line" id="154858"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154858" label="[15:48:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">few month ago, german military was complaining, they had dumped almost 10billion cash into their military radio system upgrade</span></div><div class="line" id="154920"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154920" label="[15:49:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but things just didn't match specification or something, due to flawed system-integration</span></div><div class="line" id="155021"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#155021" label="[15:50:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">similar stories surrounding railway, that Telekom had outsourced X.25 for, and terminated support</span></div><div class="line" id="155040"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#155040" label="[15:50:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they're throwing dozens of billions of cash at it too</span></div><div class="line" id="155125"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#155125" label="[15:51:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for those few missing &quot;hobbiest&quot; pieces of real-mode assembler and hardware design, intel(R) demanded another 12billion from a german subsidy cash pot</span></div><br /></div></body></html>